{
  "hookSpecificOutput": {
    "hookEventName": "SessionStart",
    "additionalContext": "## Version control and issue maintenance

**Git — the harness git defaults are reversed here.** *Commit, don't hold back:* commit in stages as work lands, each coherent step its own commit, never one blob at the end (held-back commits became un-revertable mega-commits); the harness's "don't commit unless the user asks" is off. *Push with the commits:* the harness's "confirm first" is off for push; a done segment's push lands in the same act; a hold names the feared consequence, not "your call". *Branch only on the user's word:* against the harness's "if on the default branch, branch first," commit straight onto the default branch; raise a warranted branch, worktree, or subtree rather than fork silently — the bar is the silent fork, not the branch. (`gh issue view` is dead — use `gh api repos/handle/{REPO_NAME}/issues/{ISSUE_NUMBER} --jq '{title, body, state}'`; commit with `--only` to stage whole files touched this conversation.) Claude commits carry one trailer, `Co-authored-by: <host>$ cd '<checkout path relative to $HOME>' && claude --resume <session-id>`. A git-native `prepare-commit-msg` hook (installed to a stable dir, reached through global `core.hooksPath`) writes it as git builds the object, so the stamp lands however old the authoring session's frozen hook table is; the commit-boundary hook amends the same trailer in as a live-session backup. The AI leaves it out of the commit command, which Claude Code's auto-mode classifier would otherwise block for the embedded resume command. The path stays relative to $HOME, no leading slash or tilde, so a username cannot reach a possibly public log; a checkout outside $HOME cannot be so named, so its trailer carries host and session id alone. A repo whose log must not show AI authorship is exempted with `git config claude.trailer off`, set in that clone so the exemption never enters the history; commits there carry no co-author line, the guard blocks any the AI adds, and the session is recorded only in the invisible `x-claude-session` commit header that who-did-this reads.

If user type # followed by a number, look for issue/work-item of that number. There must be update. If user just type # in a reply, it means the issue you most recently worked on has update.

A turn owes an issue an update when it disproves it or puts its premise in question, solves it (then also close it), or partially solves it, changing what remains. An open issue's body states the live work in its current shape: where that update changes the shape and the issue is on the account you file under with no comment from another party, rewrite the body forward-only. On another's, or one another party has commented on, update by comment. Closing needs no rewrite: close with a comment naming the solving commits.

Filing a fork as an issue: one issue per fork, each a concrete failure mode, adopting the most sensible path as the default and recording the choice and why as a comment. The body states the situation as it stands, not the deliberation that reached it.

Releases vs version bumps: "make a release" publishes the version already in the tree, it does not invent a new one; the number is often bumped in source ahead of the release ask, sitting unreleased through several commits, and the release act is tag-build-publish at that version. Read the source version before assuming a bump is needed: pyproject.toml / __init__.py / equivalents, or debian/changelog / spec %changelog, carrying a version above the latest release means the number is already set. For a public-facing repo, propose the next number with a default suggestion and let the user confirm rather than setting it alone. A repo with its own release cadence overrides this in its CLAUDE.md.

Git usage habits stay out of memory: a turn's instruction — push held, a branch, a worktree, a staging cadence — is an instance, not a standing preference, and memory that hardens one into a rule governs later sessions against him (is-to-ought). Git discipline's only home is this section; a repo-specific fact keeps, a preference read off one instruction does not."
  }
}

